# id: BM51-manifest-resync-blind
# blinds: a manifest edit counting as a structurally valid record after a failure
# covering-lane: cpp-test
# expect: killed
# control-lane: cpp-build
# expect-control: alive
#
# THE SUBTLEST PATCH IN THIS CATALOGUE, and it is a REVERSION rather than a
# break: it restores the WAL's own predicate exactly. Under it, nothing in a
# manifest can ever be recognised as structurally valid -- edits carry no
# sequence and manifest terminators carry zero, so `seq > committed` is false
# for everything a manifest contains.
#
# Section 5.4's discriminator then answers "no" BY CONSTRUCTION, every interior
# corruption in a manifest classifies as a torn tail, and committed manifest
# state is SILENTLY DISCARDED. A database opens cleanly having forgotten
# SSTables that are still sitting in its directory.
#
# This is the cost of reusing a reader across two file kinds, and it is the one
# thing the reuse does not give for free. It was found by asking what the shared
# predicate means for a log whose records have no sequences.
--- a/engine-cpp/src/wal/reader.cc	2026-08-25 02:31:27
+++ b/engine-cpp/src/wal/reader.cc	2026-08-25 02:31:27
@@ -98,7 +98,7 @@
   // It costs the WAL nothing: no WAL contains a manifest edit, so one appearing
   // after a corruption point is genuine interior corruption and refusing the
   // open is the right answer there too.
-  if (kind == RecordKind::kManifestEdit) return true;
+  // BM51: the WAL's predicate, unchanged, applied to both logs.
   if (kind != RecordKind::kBatch && kind != RecordKind::kGroupEnd) return false;
   return seq > committed;
 }
